This repo builds the third-party dependency bundles for every xLights desktop
platform from ONE set of submodule pins, so the platforms cannot drift:

  macOS/iOS   ./build.sh          -> xLights-macOS-dependencies.tar.zst
  Windows x64 ./build_windows.ps1 -> xLights-windows-dependencies-x64.zip

Both are attached to the same GitHub release by .github/workflows/Release.yml.


Windows notes
=============

Toolset
  Visual Studio 2026 only - VS 2022 support was dropped, so there is no
  cross-toolset ABI question to manage. env.ps1 asks vswhere for VS major >= 18
  and fails with a clear message on anything older, rather than silently
  building with VS 2022 and failing later. CI runs on the explicitly named
  windows-2025-vs2026 image (not windows-latest, which migrates between OS
  images on GitHub's schedule and would change the compiler behind a published
  artifact). BUILD_INFO.json records the exact MSVC version that produced the
  bundle. XL_VCVARS_VER can force a specific toolset when bisecting a compiler
  problem.

FFmpeg is downloaded, not built
  Everything else is compiled from the submodule pin. FFmpeg on Windows comes
  from BtbN/FFmpeg-Builds instead, because the value in FFmpeg is its
  hardware-decode matrix (nvenc/nvdec, QSV, AMF, D3D11VA, Vulkan, OpenCL) plus
  x264/x265, and maintaining that configure surface against MSYS2 is a large
  permanent burden for no gain.

  This is what xLights already does today: the avcodec.lib currently committed
  in xLights lib/windows64 is a MinGW dlltool import archive pointing at
  avcodec-60.dll, with libgcc_s_seh-1.dll / libstdc++-6.dll /
  libwinpthread-1.dll alongside it in bin64. MSVC links these import libraries
  (verified). So the change is like-for-like.

  Because the pin is a filename rather than a submodule SHA, nothing
  structurally forces it to match the macOS leg -- so FFMPEG_VERSION at the
  repo root is the single pin and build_windows.ps1 FAILS if the ffmpeg
  submodule (which the macOS leg compiles) is on a different tag.

Windows build prerequisites
  Visual Studio (2022 or newer) supplies cl, msbuild, cmake and ninja - env.ps1
  imports the dev environment and finds them inside the VS install, so no
  separate cmake/ninja install is needed. Additionally required:
    * git
    * a real Python 3, for glslang's update_glslang_sources.py. NOTE the
      WindowsApps\python.exe "app execution alias" is NOT Python - it exits
      9009 and points at the Store - so build_shader_translate.ps1 probes
      candidates by running them rather than trusting Get-Command. GitHub
      runners ship a real Python.

New submodule: zlib
  macOS and Linux take zlib from the system, so this repo never needed it.
  Windows has no system zlib and two things require it - libxlsxwriter links
  it, and xLights links z.lib - so zlib is now a submodule (pinned v1.3.1) and
  is built by the Windows leg only.

liquidfun needs working-tree patching
  liquidfun is a dormant Box2D fork. Two things break under a current
  toolchain, both patched in the working tree by build_liquidfun.ps1 and
  reverted afterwards:
    * it declares cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 2.8); CMake 4.3 (bundled with
      VS 2026) REMOVED compatibility with < 3.5 and CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM
      no longer rescues it.
    * it appends "/W4 /WX" to CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS *after* the command line, so
      warnings-as-errors cannot be turned off with -D flags.
  The macOS leg will hit the first of these once its cmake reaches 4.3; the
  build_liquidfun.sh flags.make hack already works around the second.

TLS on Apple platforms: OpenSSL + Apple SecTrust
  macOS and iOS previously used curl's SecureTransport backend, which caps the
  connection at TLS 1.2. That is not a configuration choice - Apple never added
  TLS 1.3 to SecureTransport, and curl's backend silently downgrades:

      case CURL_SSLVERSION_MAX_TLSv1_3:
      case CURL_SSLVERSION_MAX_TLSv1_2:
        ver_max = kTLSProtocol12;

  curl then removed the backend outright in 8.15.0, which is what previously
  capped the curl pin at 8.14.x.

  Both are now solved the same way: OpenSSL supplies TLS, and
  --with-apple-sectrust delegates certificate verification to the OS keychain,
  so nothing has to ship or refresh a CA bundle. OpenSSL specifically because
  curl wires SecTrust into openssl.c and gtls.c ONLY - mbedTLS or wolfSSL would
  have forced a bundled CA store, and GnuTLS is LGPL with heavier dependencies.
  OpenSSL 3.x is Apache-2.0, which is clean for App Store distribution.
  --with-apple-sectrust requires curl >= 8.18.

  Verified with live handshakes rather than by reading configure output:

      libcurl/8.21.0 OpenSSL/3.6.4
      default                 http=200  negotiated=TLSv1.3
      min TLS1.3              http=200  negotiated=TLSv1.3
      max TLS1.2 (control)    http=200  negotiated=TLSv1.2

  The negotiated version comes from SSL_get_version() via CURLINFO_TLS_SSL_PTR,
  so it reports what the connection actually used. Windows is unaffected - it
  uses Schannel, which does TLS 1.3 natively and takes trust from the OS store.

  OpenSSL costs roughly 20 MB of static libraries per platform tree, which
  reaches the iPad app as well.

Known version skew to resolve
  These are the reasons this repo exists; each needs a decision, not a silent
  workaround:

    ffmpeg  RESOLVED - both legs are on n8.1 (submodule + FFMPEG_VERSION),
            and build_windows.ps1 fails the build if they ever diverge.
            Linux still uses distro FFmpeg (Ubuntu 24.04 = 6.1, newer =
            7.x/8.x) and cannot be pinned, so xLights supports the 6.x/7.x/8.x
            range; src-core/media/FFmpegCompat.h declares that floor and
            #errors on FFmpeg 5.x or older.
    lua     macOS builds 5.4.8; xLights Windows links lua5.3.5-static.lib;
            Linux uses distro liblua5.3-dev. The Windows bundle emits lua.lib,
            so xLights' #pragma comment(lib, "lua5.3.5-static.lib") must be
            updated -- and the 5.3 -> 5.4 API change checked.
    wx      macOS pins xlights_2026.13; xLights win_CI.yml builds wxWidgets
            from HEAD. The bundle removes that by shipping the pinned build.

  postproc: xLights links postproc.lib, but BtbN's gpl-shared build does not
  ship libpostproc. Either the pragma is vestigial and should be dropped, or a
  build that includes it is needed.

  curl: xLights links "libcurl.dll.a" - a MinGW import library - and carries
  libgcc_s_seh-1.dll / libstdc++-6.dll / libwinpthread-1.dll in bin64 to
  satisfy it. The bundle's MSVC build produces libcurl.lib + libcurl.dll and
  needs none of those three, so switching means updating that pragma and
  deleting three DLLs.


Submodule versions
==================

`git submodule status` is the authority - this table is a convenience and can
drift. Regenerate it when bumping pins.

  wxWidgets     xlights_2026.13               xLights fork; carries patches - upgrade deliberately
  ffmpeg        n8.1                          macOS builds this; Windows downloads the same tag (FFMPEG_VERSION)
  SDL           release-2.32.10               SDL2 line. SDL3 is an API migration, not a bump
  curl          curl-8_21_0                   TLS via OpenSSL + Apple SecTrust (see above)
  openssl       openssl-3.6.4                 Apple platforms only; Windows uses Schannel
  zstd          v1.5.7                        current
  zlib          v1.3.2                        Windows only; macOS and Linux use the system zlib
  lua           v5.4.8                        5.5 is available but is an API break; consumers are still inconsistent
  libxlswriter  v1.2.4                        current
  hidapi        hidapi-0.15.0                 current; used for 3D mouse support on macOS and Windows
  glslang       16.4.0                        = vulkan-sdk-1.4.357.0, matched to SPIRV-Cross
  SPIRV-Cross   vulkan-sdk-1.4.357.0          matched to glslang via the same Vulkan SDK release
  liquidfun     v1.1.0-45-g7f204021           upstream dead since 2014; pin is past the last release

  ispc          v1.31.0                       installed via script, not a submodule
